<item>
  <title>Fitting a BYM2 model in R-INLA, start to finish</title>
  <dc:creator>Tareq Al-Ahdal</dc:creator>
  <link>https://alahdalta1.github.io/posts/bym2-inla/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 




<section id="why-bym2" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="why-bym2">Why BYM2</h2>
<p>When you model disease counts across regions, neighbouring areas tend to look alike. The Besag–York–Mollié (BYM) model captures this by splitting the area-level random effect into two parts: a <strong>spatially structured</strong> component (areas borrow strength from their neighbours) and an <strong>unstructured</strong> component (independent area noise).</p>
<p>The classic BYM has a practical problem — the two variances are not separately identifiable, so priors are hard to set. The <strong>BYM2</strong> reparameterization fixes this with a single precision and a mixing parameter <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cphi%20%5Cin%20%5B0,1%5D"> that says <em>how much</em> of the variance is spatial.</p>
</section>
