<section id="reading-the-mixing-parameter" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="reading-the-mixing-parameter">Reading the mixing parameter</h2>
<p>The number you care about most is the posterior of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cphi">. Close to 1 means the variation is mostly spatial (strong neighbourhood structure); close to 0 means it’s mostly independent noise.</p>
<div class="cell">
<div class="code-copy-outer-scaffold"><div class="sourceCode cell-code" id="cb2" style="background: #f1f3f5;"><pre class="sourceCode r code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode r"><span id="cb2-1">fit<span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">$</span>summary.hyperpar[<span class="st" style="color: #20794D;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">"Phi for area_id"</span>, <span class="fu" style="color: #4758AB;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">c</span>(<span class="st" style="color: #20794D;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">"mean"</span>, <span class="st" style="color: #20794D;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">"0.025quant"</span>, <span class="st" style="color: #20794D;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">"0.975quant"</span>)]</span></code></pre></div></div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="takeaways" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="takeaways">Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Use <code>scale.model = TRUE</code> so the PC priors mean the same thing across maps.</li>
<li>Report the posterior of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cphi"> — it’s the headline of a BYM2 fit.</li>
<li>The offset (<code>E</code>) is expected counts, not population; get that right first.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>This is an example post showing how code, math, and prose render. Replace it with your own tutorials.</em></p>


</section>

